1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,600
This is Theo.

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:04,320
Theo moved to Crumb Island three months ago.

3
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:06,960
And the longest conversation he's had since then...

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:08,430
was with a seagull.

5
00:00:08,711 --> 00:00:09,561
ANA: Morning!

6
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:10,737
This is Ana.

7
00:00:10,757 --> 00:00:13,057
Starting today, Ana is going to say hello to

8
00:00:13,077 --> 00:00:13,777
Theo.

9
00:00:13,797 --> 00:00:14,657
Once a day.

10
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:15,967
For thirty days.

11
00:00:16,103 --> 00:00:17,123
No speeches.

12
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:18,403
No fixing him.

13
00:00:18,423 --> 00:00:19,763
Just one hello.

14
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:22,003
Oh, and keep an eye on that calendar on

15
00:00:22,023 --> 00:00:23,203
Theo's wall.

16
00:00:23,223 --> 00:00:24,483
Because on day thirty,

17
00:00:24,503 --> 00:00:27,073
something happens that neither of them sees coming.

18
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:32,160
About half of adults in the US say they

19
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:33,760
experience loneliness.

20
00:00:33,780 --> 00:00:36,160
If that's you, nothing is broken.

21
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:38,480
Loneliness works a lot like hunger.

22
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,560
It's a signal that you need something.

23
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:42,030
In this case, people.

24
00:00:42,624 --> 00:00:43,474
Day one.

25
00:00:43,697 --> 00:00:43,917
ANA: Hi!

26
00:00:43,937 --> 00:00:44,907
I'm Ana.

27
00:00:45,349 --> 00:00:45,889
Nothing.

28
00:00:45,909 --> 00:00:47,119
He doesn't even look up.

29
00:00:47,456 --> 00:00:50,156
But here's what researchers think is going on inside

30
00:00:50,176 --> 00:00:51,036
Theo.

31
00:00:51,056 --> 00:00:52,956
When you've been lonely for a while,

32
00:00:52,976 --> 00:00:55,836
your brain can get extra alert to rejection.

33
00:00:55,856 --> 00:00:58,986
Like a smoke alarm that's a little too sensitive.

34
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:01,304
So a friendly hello can feel risky.

35
00:01:01,324 --> 00:01:03,464
From the outside, it looks cold.

36
00:01:03,484 --> 00:01:05,334
From the inside, it's protection.

37
00:01:05,949 --> 00:01:06,569
Day two.

38
00:01:06,589 --> 00:01:07,129
A nod.

39
00:01:07,149 --> 00:01:07,769
A tiny one.

40
00:01:07,789 --> 00:01:08,919
We're counting it.

41
00:01:09,222 --> 00:01:09,922
Day three.

42
00:01:09,942 --> 00:01:12,560
Mo has started watching from his shop window.

43
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,520
MO: Did he say it back?

44
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:14,800
He didn't say it back.

45
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:15,360
Okay.

46
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:16,230
Tomorrow.

47
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:17,595
Mo is invested.

48
00:01:18,357 --> 00:01:20,977
And Ana is nervous every single morning.

49
00:01:20,997 --> 00:01:22,257
Most of us would be.

50
00:01:22,277 --> 00:01:25,087
We expect chatting with strangers to be awkward.

51
00:01:25,292 --> 00:01:27,832
But when researchers asked commuters to chat with a

52
00:01:27,852 --> 00:01:30,552
stranger, they enjoyed the ride more.

53
00:01:30,572 --> 00:01:33,462
Even though almost everyone predicted the opposite.

54
00:01:33,713 --> 00:01:35,293
And tiny chats count.

55
00:01:35,313 --> 00:01:38,173
Even a short, warm chat with a barista left

56
00:01:38,193 --> 00:01:40,203
people in a better mood than rushing.

57
00:01:40,306 --> 00:01:41,726
MO: One soup, one sandwich,

58
00:01:41,746 --> 00:01:43,116
and how's your mum?

59
00:01:43,146 --> 00:01:43,526
ANA: Better!

60
00:01:43,546 --> 00:01:44,596
Thanks, Mo.

61
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:45,880
Theo sees that.

62
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,110
And on day seven...

63
00:01:47,717 --> 00:01:48,567
THEO: ...Hey.

64
00:01:48,997 --> 00:01:49,617
One word!

65
00:01:49,637 --> 00:01:51,567
Ana manages not to scream.

66
00:01:52,229 --> 00:01:52,769
Day ten.

67
00:01:52,789 --> 00:01:53,089
Hey.

68
00:01:53,109 --> 00:01:53,729
Day twelve.

69
00:01:53,749 --> 00:01:54,209
Hey.

70
00:01:54,229 --> 00:01:55,119
Day fourteen...

71
00:01:55,388 --> 00:01:56,408
ANA: He doesn't like me.

72
00:01:56,428 --> 00:01:58,358
I'm the annoying hello lady.

73
00:01:58,547 --> 00:02:00,367
And this is exactly where most of us would

74
00:02:00,387 --> 00:02:00,847
quit.

75
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:02,687
So let's check that calendar.

76
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:03,647
Day fifteen.

77
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:04,517
Night.

78
00:02:05,286 --> 00:02:06,146
Every hello.

79
00:02:06,166 --> 00:02:07,016
Circled.

80
00:02:07,164 --> 00:02:08,934
THEO: She remembered my name today.

81
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:11,110
Ana thinks she's annoying.

82
00:02:11,130 --> 00:02:13,590
Theo thinks she's the best part of his day.

83
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:15,190
That's the liking gap.

84
00:02:15,210 --> 00:02:16,710
After we talk to someone,

85
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:19,460
we tend to underestimate how much they liked us.

86
00:02:19,606 --> 00:02:21,746
That short hey wasn't a door closing.

87
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:23,376
It was a door that was stuck.

88
00:02:24,675 --> 00:02:25,535
Day sixteen.

89
00:02:25,555 --> 00:02:26,175
Rain.

90
00:02:26,195 --> 00:02:27,375
The bench is empty.

91
00:02:27,395 --> 00:02:28,335
Day seventeen.

92
00:02:28,355 --> 00:02:29,325
Still empty.

93
00:02:29,619 --> 00:02:30,079
ANA: Huh.

94
00:02:30,099 --> 00:02:31,309
I actually miss him.

95
00:02:31,688 --> 00:02:33,908
Turns out, the hellos had become the best part

96
00:02:33,928 --> 00:02:35,538
of Ana's morning too.

97
00:02:35,693 --> 00:02:37,033
She almost doesn't go.

98
00:02:37,053 --> 00:02:38,313
What if it's weird?

99
00:02:38,333 --> 00:02:40,473
But studies find that when we reach out to

100
00:02:40,493 --> 00:02:42,793
someone, they appreciate it a lot more than we

101
00:02:42,813 --> 00:02:43,663
expect.

102
00:02:43,737 --> 00:02:44,677
MO: Take the soup.

103
00:02:44,697 --> 00:02:46,227
Soup is never weird.

104
00:02:48,839 --> 00:02:48,979
THEO: You...

105
00:02:48,999 --> 00:02:50,129
noticed I was gone?

106
00:02:50,505 --> 00:02:51,715
ANA: Of course I noticed.

107
00:02:52,751 --> 00:02:54,811
After that, the hellos get longer.

108
00:02:54,831 --> 00:02:55,851
A question.

109
00:02:55,871 --> 00:02:57,371
A story about the seagull.

110
00:02:57,391 --> 00:02:58,601
An actual laugh.

111
00:02:58,927 --> 00:03:00,507
Quick reality check, though.

112
00:03:00,527 --> 00:03:03,707
One study estimated it takes roughly fifty hours together

113
00:03:03,727 --> 00:03:05,387
to become casual friends.

114
00:03:05,407 --> 00:03:08,267
By day twenty-nine, Ana and Theo are at about

115
00:03:08,287 --> 00:03:08,987
three.

116
00:03:09,007 --> 00:03:10,267
Not best friends.

117
00:03:10,287 --> 00:03:11,467
And that's okay.

118
00:03:11,487 --> 00:03:13,627
Friendship isn't one big moment.

119
00:03:13,647 --> 00:03:15,417
It's hours, stacked up.

120
00:03:15,752 --> 00:03:17,652
So if you're the Ana in your story,

121
00:03:17,672 --> 00:03:20,372
the second hello matters more than the first.

122
00:03:20,392 --> 00:03:22,802
And they probably like you more than you think.

123
00:03:23,064 --> 00:03:24,244
And if you're the Theo,

124
00:03:24,264 --> 00:03:26,244
that guarded feeling isn't a flaw.

125
00:03:26,264 --> 00:03:28,164
It's your brain protecting you.

126
00:03:28,184 --> 00:03:29,794
One word back is enough.

127
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:31,799
Day thirty.

128
00:03:31,819 --> 00:03:33,159
Ana walks to the bench...

129
00:03:33,179 --> 00:03:34,629
and Theo isn't there.

130
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:36,150
He's across the square.

131
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:37,270
Next to Finn.

132
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:39,780
Finn, who's been sitting alone all week.

133
00:03:40,243 --> 00:03:40,543
THEO: Morning.

134
00:03:40,563 --> 00:03:41,613
I'm Theo.

135
00:03:42,422 --> 00:03:44,642
Thirty days ago, Theo couldn't look up.

136
00:03:44,662 --> 00:03:46,832
Today, he's somebody's day one.

137
00:03:47,888 --> 00:03:49,328
Maybe you're somebody's Ana.
